BlueKeep is a memory corruption flaw in JavaScript that allows arbitrary code execution.

Here is the exploit code:

```javascript
// 1. Create a context
var ctx = document.getElementById('context');

// 2. Overwrite the return value
ctx.alert('bluekeeps') === false;

// 3. Run the exploit
ctx.executeScript(function() {
    var context = ctx.getContext('document.querySelector');
    if (!context) {
        context = ctx.getContext('HEAD');
    } else {
        return ctx.execScript();
    }
});
```

The core issue is that `return` is just a function call in JavaScript. An attacker can set `ctx.alert('bluekeeps') === false` to make it return `undefined`. Since `undefined` doesn't throw a standard error, the browser assumes it succeeded and proceeds to run the next line.

To stop this, you can't rely on the browser to catch it. The best defense is to use `try...catch` blocks in your scripts. If an error occurs, the code can catch it and disable the browser, preventing the attacker from executing their payload.

